**What**
`git store` covers put/get/list/log/rm plus a `schema` subgroup, and the
vocabulary is well chosen. Three capabilities that the data model already
supports have no command, and all output except `get` is untyped text.

**Where**
`crates/git-store/src/main.rs:34-102` (the full command tree),
`main.rs:397-404` (`print_log`), `main.rs:324-328` (`print_lines`).

**Why it matters**

*No way to ask which schema a value uses.* This is the systems most
distinctive property - every commit pins its schema - and it is unreachable
from the CLI. Today `Store::retrieve_at` reads the `Schema:` trailer
(`crates/gix-store/src/store.rs:204`) and throws the schema commit away. Once
`0b4a9b27` lands and schema binding moves from trailer to subtree, this
becomes reading the `schema/` subtree of the data commit instead of scraping a
trailer - a new `gix-store` accessor, not a text parse. Either way, a user who
wants to know why `carbonara` has a numeric `serves` while `schema show` says
`string` has to run `git log --format=%B` and read a trailer by hand today. A
`git store schema of <kind> <name> [rev]` would surface the best idea in the
project.

*No diff.* `git store log recipe carbonara` gives oids and dates; there is no
`git store diff` and, per the encoding issues filed separately, raw `git diff`
is unreliable for enums and options. The tool that stores versioned structured
data cannot show what changed between two versions.

*Output is not machine-readable, and `log` drops the message.* `print_log`
prints `<oid> <iso-date>` - the commit summary, which is the only human-authored
content in the record and the thing `-m` exists to set, is discarded:

```
$ git store log recipe carbonara
b67d0ea476b77174a0cd7516041921292129ac25 2026-07-28 09:46:03 +0900
931c49a9172262b63f65ee8162de4eec34883828 2026-07-28 09:45:53 +0900
```

Two space-separated columns where the second contains spaces, so `cut -d(space)
-f2` does not work. There is no `--format json` anywhere; `list`, `log`, and
`schema show` are all bespoke text. The project pitches composability with the
Unix toolbox, and `get` composes with `jq` beautifully, but nothing else does.

*Minor but related:* `git store list nosuchkind` exits 0 with no output, so a
script cannot distinguish "kind has no entities" from "kind does not exist".

**Options**
- Option A - add `git store schema of <kind> <name> [rev]`, `git store diff
  <kind> <name> [revA] [revB]`, and a global `--format=text|json`. Roughly one
  new subcommand each and a serializer switch; `diff` can be a value-level
  comparison of two decoded `Value`s, which is straightforward and reads far
  better than the tree diff. `schema of` needs a new `gix-store` accessor
  either way - reading a trailer today, reading the `schema/` subtree once
  `0b4a9b27` lands - since `retrieve_at` currently discards the schema
  entirely.
- Option B - add only `--format json` and lean on `git` itself for the rest
  (`git log --format=%B` for the trailer, `git diff` for changes). Trade-off:
  ideologically consistent with "git is the query language", but per the
  empty-tree issue `git diff` currently does not deliver on enums and options,
  so this bets on that being fixed first.
- Option C - add `git store diff` only, as the demo safety net.

**Recommendation**
Option A, high confidence on the value of `schema of` in particular - it is a
handful of lines, it makes an invisible design decision visible, and it is the
command that turns "every commit pins its schema" from a bullet point into
something you can type on stage. `--format json` is table stakes for a tool
that expects to be scripted. This item is blocked by `0b4a9b27` (Wave 1): the
`schema of` accessor should be built against the subtree-binding design so it
is not written once against the trailer and rewritten once the binding
changes underneath it.